{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartBEulerTail","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartBEulerTail","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.GeomSum","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Normed.Group.Tannery","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.ZetaValues"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_asymptotic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_asymptotic","kind":"theorem","line":222,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBEulerTail.lean#L222-L260","doc":"**V2 (capstone) — the Euler-function / Dedekind-eta asymptotic (`W-P2`).**  The\nnegative-factor Euler tail, rescaled by `(1−p)`, converges to `ζ(2) = π²/6`:\n\n`(1−p)·Σ'_m −log(1−p^{m+1}) → π²/6`   as `p → 1⁻`,\n\nequivalently `(1−p)·log ∏_{m≥1}(1−pᵐ) → −π²/6` (the classical Hardy–Ramanujan\neta asymptotic).  Assembles V2a (`euler_tail_lambert_rearrange`) with V2b\n(`euler_tail_jseries_tendsto`): the `(1−p)`-scaled Lambert `j`-series is the\nV2b summand reindexed by the vanishing `j=0` term.  With `p = 1/q`\n(`q→1⁺ ⟺ p→1⁻`) this is exactly the regularized negative-factor piece of\nPart B (`W-P2 → −π²/6`), one of the two halves of `Li₂(x) − π²/3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_lambert_rearrange","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_lambert_rearrange","kind":"theorem","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBEulerTail.lean#L158-L220","doc":"**V2a — the Lambert rearrangement.**  For `0 < p < 1`, the negative-factor\nEuler tail (in tsum-of-logs form) equals its Lambert `j`-series:\n\n`Σ'_m −log(1−p^{m+1}) = Σ'_j (1/(j+1))·p^{j+1}/(1−p^{j+1})`.\n\nA nonneg Fubini (`Summable.tsum_comm`) on `a m j = p^{(m+1)(j+1)}/(j+1)`:\nthe row sum is `−log(1−p^{m+1})` (`neg_log_one_sub_hasSum`), the column sum is\nthe geometric `(1/(j+1))·p^{j+1}/(1−p^{j+1})`.  Combined with\n`euler_tail_jseries_tendsto` this yields the eta asymptotic\n`(1−p)·Σ'_m −log(1−p^{m+1}) → π²/6`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_jseries_tendsto","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_jseries_tendsto","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBEulerTail.lean#L112-L156","doc":"**V2 (heart) — the Lambert `j`-series limit.**  The negative-factor Euler tail\nin its Lambert form converges to `ζ(2) = π²/6` as `p → 1⁻`:\n\n`Σ_{j} (1/j)·(1−p)·pʲ/(1−pʲ) → Σ_{j} 1/j² = π²/6`.\n\nThis is Tannery's theorem (`tendsto_tsum_of_dominated_convergence`) applied to\nthe two V1 inputs: termwise `(1/j)·(1−p)pʲ/(1−pʲ) → 1/j²`\n(`euler_tail_coeff_limit`), uniformly dominated by the summable `1/j²`\n(`euler_tail_coeff_le` + `euler_tail_coeff_nonneg`), with `Σ 1/j² = π²/6`\n(`hasSum_zeta_two`).  The Lambert rearrangement\n`−log ∏(1−pᵐ) = Σⱼ (1/j)·pʲ/(1−pʲ)` (which turns this into the eta asymptotic\n`(1−p)·log ∏(1−pᵐ) → −π²/6`) is the remaining W-P2 step. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_coeff_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_coeff_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBEulerTail.lean#L105-L110","doc":"Positivity of the `j`-th Lambert coefficient for `0 < p < 1`, `j ≥ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_coeff_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_coeff_le","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBEulerTail.lean#L75-L103","doc":"**V1 — Euler-tail coefficient uniform bound.**  For `0 < p < 1` and `j ≥ 1`,\n`(1−p)·pʲ/(1−pʲ) ≤ 1/j`.  Together with `euler_tail_coeff_limit` (the termwise\nlimit `→ 1/j`) this is the second Tannery/dominated-convergence input for\n`W-P2`: the coefficient times `1/j` is dominated by the summable `1/j²`.\nProof: `(1−p)pʲ/(1−pʲ) = pʲ/Σ_{i<j}pⁱ`, and each of the `j` summands\n`pⁱ (i<j)` is `≥ pʲ`, so `Σ_{i<j}pⁱ ≥ j·pʲ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_coeff_limit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.euler_tail_coeff_limit","kind":"theorem","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBEulerTail.lean#L39-L73","doc":"**V1 — Euler-tail termwise coefficient limit.**  As `p → 1⁻`, the `j`-th\nLambert coefficient `(1−p)·pʲ/(1−pʲ)` tends to `1/j`.  Since\n`1 − pʲ = (1−p)·Σ_{i<j} pⁱ`, the `(1−p)` cancels, leaving `pʲ/Σ_{i<j}pⁱ`,\nwhose limit at `p=1` is `1/j` (numerator `→ 1`, denominator `→ j`).  This is\nthe termwise input to the Tannery/DCT giving the negative-factor Euler tail\n`(1−p)·log ∏(1−pᵐ) → −π²/6` (Part B, `W-P2`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}